WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.210 align:start position:0%
 
let me state the obvious the situation  in Greece is very fluid we've had ups  and downs but a real intensification as  talks broke off but in the weekend and  this referendum has been called we've  had the the Greek state going into  arrears with the IMF yesterday so and  obviously the bank holidays there so  without question in Greece the situation  has intensified and as a product of that  it raises the possibility of some  contagion more broadly into European  economies and within financial markets  so that's the overall risks and our job  is to worry about risk it also is to put  in place the protections against those  risks and it won't surprise you that we  knew that there was this possibility and  we have been preparing for it now so why  is the the direct impact on the United  Kingdom small well first thing is  there's very little exposure of UK banks  to two Greek entities including the  Greek state secondly there's there's  hardly any activity of Greek banks here  in the UK it's tiny relative to our  economy and thirdly we have been working  with our European partners in part to  help them put in place over the last  number of years much more protections to  prevent that so-called contagion that  jumping of risks in Greece into the core  of continental Europe through improving  the banking system through improving the  toolkit for the European Central Bank  and other facilities for European states  to draw on to limit limit this contagion  and that time has been well spent those  defenses are quite strong they might be  tested in coming days and weeks and  months but there are good reasons to  think not just that the direct exposure  to Greece is quite low but this indirect  route can be managed it looks this  morning or so the Greeks may have  compromised  to an extent do you take encouragement  from that presumably the best thing  would be if we could revive some sort of  deal and not have a referendum  weekend well it's as I say it's a  fast-moving situation it is in the best  interests of all the parties to come to  an agreement both the Greek authorities  and in the European authorities and  we'll do whatever we can to help that  and we certainly urge them to do so but  it's complicated it's complicated by  past decisions it's complicated by the  facts on the ground it's complicated by  issues around debt and the structural  reforms we don't want to get in the way  of those discussions and what we would  just encourage them to move to  conclusion as quickly as possible  without getting too technical what are  the contingency plans that you've drawn  up to limit contagion well one of the  one of the issues is just to make sure  that the Greek banks and and  subsidiaries that operate here in the UK  have the liquidity that that they need  to operate and if they don't that they  would they would be be be shot and so  for the Greek banks that's the  responsibility of the Greek authorities  we have some oversight of them and we  have oversight of liquidity and their  liquidity is held here at the Bank of  England so we take those  responsibilities for the Greek there's  one's Greek subsidiary banking  subsidiary operates here we're their  supervisor so we've made sure over the  last few years they have a lot of  capital a lot of liquidity and we have a  good daily view into all of that and and  so those aspects of heightened  monitoring and oversight and management  that is in place for the Greek banks we  coordinate with the Greek authorities  and the European authorities for the  subsidiaries our direct responsibility  just to be absolutely clear so there's  that element of it secondly there are a  series of conversations that we have  with the European Central Bank other  European banking authorities that with  the Treasury here and by the Treasury  European treasurer's to make sure that  broader contingency plans are ready and  and and those contingencies could be put  in place if necessary quite frankly they  haven't been necessary because we  haven't seen this spillover of the risks  in Greece into into broader financial  markets or into broader Europe but again  our job is to make sure that they're  ready I don't really want to  go through all the technicalities of  that not just because it's too technical  but also it makes them less effective  but you can be assured that we have been  preparing for this possibility and thus  far the coordination both here in the UK  in my view is exemplary and with our  European partners is what you would  expect is there anything you would have  done differently had you been in Mario  Draghi issues over the last few weeks  that there's nothing I would have done  differently if I've been in issues over  the last few years no I think President  Draghi in his colleagues have have very  importantly put in place not just  specific decisions but the actual tools  that they've put in place their ability  to buy sovereign bonds their bill in  order to provide stimulus to the  European economy just as the Bank of  England did several years ago provide  stimulus to this economy that's hugely  important but also the ability to take  action to protect the so-called  integrity of the euro to to ground  action in in keeping the euro together  that's a hugely important because people  in financial markets know that they will  act and the consequence of that is is  not to speculate against challenges  within the Europe so he's he and his  colleagues have done the right things  they have worked very hard to continue  to provide liquidity to the Greek  banking system they continued to do that  they haven't been increasing but they've  continued to provide liquidity so we're  fully supportive of the actions that he  and others that the ECB have taken now  I've seen the financial stability report  published today Greece is only one of a  number of potential risks to the system  that you outline I almost came away with  the impression that we should be more I  worried about China than Greece well I  think the China obviously second largest  economy in the world our banks and this  is a good news story you have much  greater exposure to China than they do  to Greece they just give you the numbers  if I may the Greek Greek exposures about  1% of their common equity Chinese  exposure is three and a half times their  common equity Chinese and other emerging  markets I should say I should be clear  it's a broader set now that's a good  thing because that's faster part a  growing part of the world and as part of  being global banks but it does me  that slowing Chinese economy a  restructuring in the Chinese economy  which is what is happening right now has  some implications through our financial  system and ultimately through demand for  a British product so it is important now  this is these are more medium term  issues Greece's you know this the  crystallization of wrist around graces  is an immediate issue it's changing by  the hour we're very focused on that  issues in China are more to the  medium-term they're more related to the  opening up of that economy the  development of that economy but yes in  the fullness of time  China's absolutely more in an order of  magnitude of magnitude more important  than developments on the periphery of  Europe now another risk that you've  mentioned is in the housing market and  specifically the buy-to-let market the  policy responses required here to calm  down that market that go beyond the Bank  of England's remit well a year ago we  sat down and and we had taken some  measures on owner-occupied housing so  you know that element of the market what  we and what we had done there was to  take out insurance we hadn't restricted  things that were actually happening but  we had taken measures to in effect  prevent banks from doing what they  normally do in the past which is to move  from responsible underwriting standards  to reckless underwriting standards and  we all end up paying for it in the end  so we've taken out insurance against  that owner-occupied and we've kept that  insurance in place and that's the right  thing to do and we've seen some positive  developments in the housing market as  consequence of that then at that time we  decided not to take measures against  buy-to-let partly given the relative  size that buy-to-let partly because of  the better underwriting standards at the  time and vital that variety of factors  we have we've discussed it again at this  meeting and we again decided not to take  any action we could take action with  respect to buy-to-let because it is  within our remit if there's an activity  that could affect financial stability  United Kingdom we can make  recommendations to the relevant bodies  to take action in fact that's what we  did a year ago with owner  you find we made a recommendation we  didn't have direct responsibility we  made a recommendation to the PRA which  is a group that regulates the banks and  the FCA which regulates sort of non-bank  providers of mortgages and that worked  and so if we needed to do something for  buy-to-let we could make a  recommendation the judgment of the  committee is we do not need to do  something at this stage right now you  also talked a lot about misconduct among  banks a couple of weeks ago  mansion-house you were indicating that  you thought the relationship between the  banking sector and society should be  reset and yet in the financial stability  report it points out that there is deep  mistrust towards the banking sector  particularly from SMEs well essentially  Ian when I you know one of the pleasures  of this job is I get to go around this  country and meet businesses and people  throughout the United Kingdom I have to  say in every region every time I go  outside the City of London which is  frequently one of the questions I'm  asked is about the banking system it's  often quite blunt why haven't certain  you know why haven't bankers gone to  jail what's going on in the city there's  a series of scandals and and and the  reality is that this this series of  misconduct has undermined trust in the  system and it's overshadowed what is a  very good news story which is the  improvement in the capital liquidity the  overall strength of the banking system  and a good news story which is  incredibly relevant to what we've been  discussing in terms of risk from Greece  or China or the housing market it  matters that our system is stronger so  we have to fix this we have to fix these  issues and with respect to misconduct  and it the answer isn't just to find the  institutions there have been huge fines  30 billion pounds worth of fines on UK  institutions that's equivalent to all  the capital they've raised in privately  since 2009 it's about shifting greater  responsibility to individuals - so I'm  able to have better answer those  questions that I hear around the country  why have an individual's taken  responsibility for the mistakes that  have been made not just mistakes but  outright misconduct in these  institutions and and mansion-house and  the associated reports around that show  way to rebalance responsibility to  individuals so that we have a a system  that merits the trust of the people now  in the report you call for more  monitoring of cyber risks and an  improvement of resilience in the system  do you think the current financial  system is sufficiently resilient to  withstand cyber attack no well you know  I think that any complacency like that  is is a recipe for that risk  crystallizing in other words you this is  a constantly evolving risk these whether  it's state actors or or private  cyberattacks you you have to prepare for  them so what we've done is we've  developed along with industry a  penetration testing mechanism so a way  of testing the resilience of banks  including the Bank of England we've been  subjected to it but how well are you  protected against cyber risk and this  isn't just that I think the key thing  I'd leave you with is it's not just  about the technical aspect of it  technical defences is but it's it's how  senior management is seized with these  issues because you have to understand  the nature of attacks you have to build  not just resilience but your ability to  recover from attacks and you have to do  it constantly there has to be one of the  questions that the chief executive  officer and the board are constantly  asking themselves or you won't be ready  so we're trying to play our role in  helping that along but I wouldn't be  complacent at all about the resilience  of the system to cyber because as soon  as you become complacent itself defeat

